:39,"_42":120,"_44":39,"_45":39,"_46":121,"_48":122,"_50":123,"_52":124,"_54":125,"_56":-5,"_57":126,"_59":127,"_61":128,"_63":129,"_65":39,"_66":67},{"_26":100,"_28":101,"_30":102,"_32":103,"_34":104,"_36":105,"_38":39,"_40":39,"_42":84,"_44":39,"_45":39,"_46":47,"_48":86,"_50":106,"_52":107,"_54":108,"_56":-5,"_57":109,"_59":110,"_61":111,"_63":112,"_65":39,"_66":67},{"_26":80,"_28":81,"_30":31,"_32":82,"_34":83,"_36":84,"_38":39,"_40":39,"_42":85,"_44":39,"_45":39,"_46":86,"_48":47,"_50":87,"_52":88,"_54":89,"_56":-5,"_57":90,"_59":91,"_61":92,"_63":93,"_65":39,"_66":67},{"_26":27,"_28":29,"_30":31,"_32":33,"_34":35,"_36":37,"_38":39,"_40":41,"_42":43,"_44":39,"_45":41,"_46":47,"_48":49,"_50":51,"_52":53,"_54":55,"_56":-5,"_57":58,"_59":60,"_61":62,"_63":64,"_65":39,"_66":67},"id","2252","name","Edo Period","type","period","startDescription","The Edo Period was a time of peace and stability under the Tokugawa shogunate in Japan, marked by economic growth, isolationist foreign policy, and flourishing arts and culture.","endDescription","It ended with the Meiji Restoration, which restored imperial rule and modernized Japan.","startYear",1603,"startMonth",0,"startDay",1,"endYear",1868,"endMonth","endDay","startSignificance",0.8,"endSignificance",0.85,"imageUrl","https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Edo_Period_map.png/330px-Edo_Period_map.png","imageCredit","Image: Edo Period / Wikimedia Commons","sourceUrl","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edo_period","cdnId","country",{"_28":77,"_78":79},"subjects",[74],"group",{"_28":71,"_72":73},"user",{"_26":68,"_69":70},"likes","liked",false,"6","username","HistOracle","Classical Japan","icon","GiJapaneseBridge",{"_26":75,"_28":76},"3","culture","Japan","continent","Asia","1673","Kamakura Shogunate","This was the first shogunate in Japan, marking the rise of military governance and the samurai class.","The shogunate ended with the fall of Kamakura, leading to a shift in power dynamics in Japan.",1185,1333,0.9,"","Image: Kamakura shogunate / Wikimedia Commons","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamakura_shogunate",{"_28":77,"_78":79},[94,95],{"_28":71,"_72":73},{"_26":68,"_69":70},{"_26":98,"_28":99},{"_26":96,"_28":97},"2","politics","1","military","1409","The Genpei War","event","A significant conflict in Japanese history, marking the struggle between the Taira and Minamoto clans for control of the imperial throne.","The war concluded with the defeat of the Taira clan, leading to the establishment of the Kamakura shogunate.",1180,"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Genpei_kassen.jpg/330px-Genpei_kassen.jpg","Image: Genpei War / Wikimedia Commons","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genpei_War",{"_28":77,"_78":79},[113],{"_28":71,"_72":73},{"_26":68,"_69":70},{"_26":98,"_28":99},"1837","Hōnen","person","A prominent Buddhist monk known for founding the Jōdo-shū sect, emphasizing the importance of faith in Amida Buddha.","His teachings significantly influenced Japanese Buddhism and spirituality.",1133,1212,0.7,0.5,"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Takanobu-no-miei.jpg/330px-Takanobu-no-miei.jpg","Image: Hōnen / Wikimedia Commons","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C5%8Dnen",{"_28":77,"_78":79},[130,131],{"_28":71,"_72":73},{"_26":68,"_69":70},{"_26":134,"_28":135},{"_26":132,"_28":133},"7","religion","4","intellectual","1834","Murasaki Shikibu","An influential writer and poet, she is best known for her work 'The Tale of Genji', considered the world's first novel.","Her contributions to literature have had a lasting impact on Japanese culture and storytelling.",973,1014,"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Murasaki-Shikibu-composing-Genji-Monogatari.png/330px-Murasaki-Shikibu-composing-Genji-Monogatari.png","Image: Murasaki Shikibu / Wikimedia Commons","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murasaki_Shikibu",{"_28":77,"_78":79},[149,150],{"_28":71,"_72":73},{"_26":68,"_69":70},{"_26":75,"_28":76},{"_26":134,"_28":135},"1343","Heian Period","This era marked a time of cultural flourishing and the establishment of a distinct Japanese identity, characterized by the development of art, literature, and courtly life.","The conclusion of this period saw the rise of military power and the beginning of feudalism, leading to significant changes in governance and society.",794,"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Map-of-Japan-1183-Heian-Genpei-War.png/330px-Map-of-Japan-1183-Heian-Genpei-War.png","Image: Heian period / Wikimedia Commons","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heian_period",{"_28":77,"_78":79},[163,164],{"_28":71,"_72":73},{"_26":68,"_69":70},{"_26":75,"_28":76},{"_26":165,"_28":166},"9","art","1838","Kōbō Daishi","A revered Buddhist monk and founder of the Shingon sect, he played a crucial role in the spread of esoteric Buddhism in Japan.","His legacy continues to influence Japanese spirituality and culture.",774,835,"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Eight_Patriarchs_of_the_Shingon_Sect_of_Buddhism_Kukai_Cropped.jpg/330px-Eight_Patriarchs_of_the_Shingon_Sect_of_Buddhism_Kukai_Cropped.jpg","Image: Kūkai / Wikimedia Commons","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%C5%8Db%C5%8D_Daishi",{"_28":77,"_78":79},[180,181],{"_28":71,"_72":73},{"_26":68,"_69":70},{"_26":75,"_28":76},{"_26":132,"_28":133},"1678","Tōdai-ji","landmark","This historic temple complex is known for its Great Buddha statue and significant role in Japanese Buddhism.","It remains an important cultural and religious site, attracting visitors and scholars alike.",752,"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/T%C5%8Ddai-ji_Kon-d%C5%8D.jpg/330px-T%C5%8Ddai-ji_Kon-d%C5%8D.jpg","Image: Tōdai-ji / Wikimedia Commons","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/T%C5%8Ddai-ji",{"_28":77,"_78":79},[195],{"_28":71,"_72":73},{"_26":68,"_69":70},{"_26":132,"_28":133},"1677","Nara Period","This era marked the establishment of the first permanent capital in Japan, leading to significant cultural and political developments.","The period concluded with the capital moving to a new location, signaling a shift in political power and cultural focus.",710,"Image: Nara period / Wikimedia Commons","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nara_period",{"_28":77,"_78":79},[207,208],{"_28":71,"_72":73},{"_26":68,"_69":70},{"_26":96,"_28":97},{"_26":75,"_28":76},"1566","Asuka Period","This era marked the introduction of Buddhism to Japan, influencing various aspects of culture and governance.","The period concluded with the establishment of the Nara period, which saw the centralization of power and the development of a more structured state.",538,"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Yamato_Kingship_in_East_Asia.svg/330px-Yamato_Kingship_in_East_Asia.svg.png","Image: Asuka period / Wikimedia Commons","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asuka_period",{"_28":77,"_78":79},[221,222],{"_28":71,"_72":73},{"_26":68,"_69":70},{"_26":75,"_28":76},{"_26":132,"_28":133},"1835","Yayoi Period","This era is characterized by the introduction of rice cultivation and the development of new technologies in Japan.","It laid the foundations for the subsequent Kofun period and the emergence of a more complex society.",300,"Image: Yayoi period / Wikimedia Commons","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yayoi_period",{"_28":77,"_78":79},[234,235],{"_28":71,"_72":73},{"_26":68,"_69":70},{"_26":75,"_28":76},{"_26":236,"_28":237},"8","economic",[],"subject",[],[],"yearStart",-300000,"yearEnd",2025,"search","sortBy",true,26]
